## Container build

The root `Dockerfile` builds only `{{proj_axum}}`. Prepare the workspace first:

1. Check `[workspace.dependencies]` in `Cargo.toml`. `edgezero new` may have
   generated paths into the developer's EdgeZero checkout or unpinned Git
   references. Replace every EdgeZero entry with the same approved release tag
   or commit at `https://github.com/IABTechLab/edgezero.git`, preserving its
   feature settings. Keep paths between this application's crates.
2. Run `cargo generate-lockfile`, review the dependency sources with
   `cargo metadata --locked --format-version 1`, and commit this application's
   manifests and `Cargo.lock`. Do not copy EdgeZero's lockfile. These preparation
   commands may fetch dependencies; scaffolding itself does not.
3. Keep the Docker builder's Rust pin aligned with `.tool-versions`. If adding
   `rust-toolchain.toml`, use a matching exact quoted `[toolchain].channel`.
   Update both declarations and the reviewed builder image when changing Rust.
4. Review `.dockerignore` and add your app-specific config, secrets and state
   filenames. Keep required workspace manifests and build-time assets.

```sh
docker build -t app:local .
docker run --rm --read-only --cap-drop=ALL \
  --security-opt=no-new-privileges:true \
  -p 127.0.0.1:8787:8787 app:local
```

The image runs as UID/GID 10001 and binds `0.0.0.0:8787` inside the container.
Local development still defaults to localhost. The default app has no stores
and needs no writable mount. Apps using current embedded stores must provision
an explicit `/app/.edgezero` mount with permissions for UID 10001. No config or
keys are generated by the image. Embedded redb requires one writer.

This recipe proves packaging, not production readiness or bounded SIGTERM
shutdown. Application owners publish their own images. See the
[Axum guide](https://iabtechlab.github.io/edgezero/guide/adapters/axum)
for runtime limitations, optional asset builds and container checks.

{{/if}}

## Configuration

Environment variables are declared in `edgezero.toml`. Set `API_BASE_URL` to the upstream origin you want `/proxy/...` to target; the handlers read it through `RequestContext::variable`, including from the declared Cloudflare Worker binding. Adapter-specific secrets are opt-in: uncomment the `#[secret]` field in `crates/{{proj_core}}/src/config.rs` and the matching `[stores.secrets]` block in `edgezero.toml`, then provide the secret value through the wired backend (Cloudflare Worker secret, Fastly Secret Store, Spin secret variable, ...). The Axum dev server reads secret key `demo_api_token` from the environment variable `EDGEZERO__SECRETS__DEMO_API_TOKEN`, which is the `EDGEZERO__SECRETS__` prefix plus the key in uppercase.

### Typed app config (`{{name}}.toml`)

`{{name}}.toml` maps onto `{{NameUpperCamel}}Config` in
`crates/{{proj_core}}/src/config.rs`. Handlers receive it via the
`AppConfig<{{NameUpperCamel}}Config>` extractor -- no manual config-store
or secret-store calls needed. The `{{proj_cli}}` binary wires the typed
push and validate commands:

```sh
# Validate the manifest + typed config (skips secret-field rules at
# push time; runtime validates the resolved secret values per spec 3.3.8):
cargo run -p {{proj_cli}} -- config validate

# Push `{{name}}.toml` as a single blob envelope into the adapter's
# config store. The blob carries every field verbatim -- including
# `#[secret]` fields, whose value at rest is the key NAME (Model A
# per spec 3.3); the runtime extractor swaps each name for the
# resolved secret value at request time.
cargo run -p {{proj_cli}} -- config push --adapter <axum|cloudflare|fastly|spin>

# Compare the local TOML against the deployed remote. `--exit-code`
# turns it into a CI gate: exit 1 if changes exist.
cargo run -p {{proj_cli}} -- config diff --adapter <name> --format json --exit-code
```

Uncomment `[stores.config]` in `edgezero.toml` (and the matching
adapter binding in the per-adapter manifest) before running `config push`.
For Axum, push before starting the server or restart after updating config: the
runner loads bounded immutable snapshots before listener binding. Configure allocation
budgets through `EDGEZERO__ADAPTER__CONFIG_STORE__*`; invalid budgets fail startup.
Raw custom store implementations return shared `edgezero_core::ConfigValue`, while
the typed config extractor and generated handlers require no manual store reads.

Use `--key <name>` for per-environment overrides (staging / canary) on adapters
that support custom runtime keys:
the same `{{name}}.toml` can land under multiple keys, and the runtime
picks one via the canonical `EDGEZERO__STORES__CONFIG__APP_CONFIG__KEY=<name>`
setting. Axum reads that setting from process env, Cloudflare from worker
vars, and Spin from application variables. Fastly uses the logical key
`app_config` for production, staging, and local Viceroy. Its deploy flow links
the selected physical store under the logical `app_config` alias; no service ID
appears in an environment variable name.
See the [blob app-config migration guide](https://iabtechlab.github.io/edgezero/guide/blob-app-config-migration)
for the per-adapter steps and the full operator runbook.
